c(ALL, 'L5',
  'Lead L-313, first item: collectProtocolFees never reads the lock and skips the synced check for address(0) (src/ProtocolFees.sol:48-52 against src/interfaces/IProtocolFees.sol:40).',
  'Answered in cycle 1 and not repeated: the cleared items for chain ids 1, 137, 56 and 5042 walked a native collection while a native face is synced and found that it lowers the settler own credit, and the critic of cycle 5 confirmed on Ethereum, Polygon and Arc that the deployed contracts collect inside an unlock. This unit added one read. The fee controllers of the eight networks that have one (chain ids 1, 10, 8453, 42161, 4663, 137, 56 and 5042) all expose the same sixteen selectors, among them 0xe0511234 for collect((address,uint256)[]) and 0x06ab95ba for triggerFeeUpdate with a PoolKey argument, and each answers POOL_MANAGER() with the PoolManager of its network and TOKEN_JAR() with one fixed address. Seven of them are 4,544 bytes long and the one on Arc is 3,480 bytes. I did not read the adapter code on the seven networks other than Ethereum, so that the recipient is fixed there rests on the getter and on the shared selector set.',
  'eth_getCode and eth_call on the rpc URLs of chain ids 1, 10, 8453, 42161, 4663, 137, 56 and 5042 on 2026-11-23, script controllers.py, results in ' + W + '/raw/controllers.json; src/ProtocolFees.sol:44-57; research/network-risk/state/ledger.json criticNotes of cycle 5')

c(ALL, 'L2',
  'sync has no lock check (src/PoolManager.sol:279-288), while src/interfaces/IPoolManager.sol:111 names initialize and updateDynamicLPFee as the only functions that run without an unlock. What can an ordinary account do with that, and which network feature turns it into a gain or a freeze for others.',
  'An ordinary account can set the synced currency and the reserves value while the manager is locked, and nothing else. On all 21 deployed PoolManagers a sync(T) from a plain contract outside any unlock succeeded for a stub T that answers balanceOf with 32 bytes. exttload then returned T in the currency slot and the answer in the reserves slot, the lock flag read 0, and sync(address(0)) cleared the currency slot. The tests of the repository expect this (test/Sync.t.sol:36-41, test/PoolManager.t.sol:1001-1008) and the test double declares sync without a modifier (src/test/ProxyPoolManager.sol:139-148), so only the interface comment disagrees. A lock check would not narrow what an account can reach. Any contract can call unlock, call sync inside its callback and return with every delta at zero (src/PoolManager.sol:104-114). On all 21 networks such an unlock closed and the synced currency stayed in place afterwards, with the lock flag and the delta counter back at 0. A later settle with value in the same call then reverted with NonzeroNativeValue, and it passed once the payer called sync(address(0)) first. That effect is finding F-071 and stays as recorded there. The other network features of the task were walked against the two slots. Code that runs between another payer transfer and its settle: the PoolManager makes no call between those two steps, so such code can come only from the token or from the payer own router. It would not need sync or the missing lock check, because settle and settleFor are open to every caller while the manager is unlocked (src/PoolManager.sol:300-307). A credit captured that way leaves the payer delta open, and the payer unlock reverts at src/PoolManager.sol:112 unless the payer code ignores the amount that settle returns. This is the same on Ethereum. A failed frame: on all 21 networks an unlock whose callback reverted left no lock flag and no delta behind, since the next unlock in the same call opened and closed normally. Transient storage across transactions and inside protocol level batches (assumption A1) was read from client sources by the units of cycle 3 and was not repeated here.',
  RPC21 + ', scripts tests.py and stale.py, results raw/tests_<chainId>.json and raw/stale.json in ' + W + '; src/PoolManager.sol:104-114, 279-288, 300-307, 349-365; test/Sync.t.sol:36-41; src/test/ProxyPoolManager.sol:139-148')

c([42220, 5042, 137], 'L3',
  'A sync of a native face made before an unlock on Celo, Arc and Polygon, where an ERC-20 address measures the native balance: can one payment be credited under address(0) and under the face, and what does a native payout do while the face is synced.',
  'No double credit on any of the three. One eth_call per network ran the walk on the deployed PoolManager at blocks 83,538,264 (Celo), 32,738,581 (Arc) and 97,822,371 (Polygon), with a funded stub as the ordinary account. With the face synced before the unlock, settle with 1000 wei reverted with NonzeroNativeValue (src/PoolManager.sol:356). After a zero value settle cleared the slot, settle with 1000 wei credited 1000 under address(0). A new sync of the face stored the higher balance and the next settle paid 0, so the face delta stayed 0 and the native delta 1000. On Celo a transfer of 1000 through the CELO token 0x471EcE3750Da237f93B8E339c536989b8978a438 was credited as 1000 under the face id, and take under that id paid 1000 wei of CELO to the recipient. On Arc one unit sent through 0x3600000000000000000000000000000000000000 was credited as 1 under the face id, and take under that id paid 10^12 wei. On Polygon the transfer through 0x0000000000000000000000000000000000001010 failed and take under that id reverted, so that id can be neither paid in nor paid out. On all three the native balance of the PoolManager ended at its starting value and every delta read 0. A native take while the face is synced made the next settle revert with a Panic on the checked subtraction (src/PoolManager.sol:360), which costs the settler and nobody else. An account whose EIP-7702 designator names 0x1010 on Polygon also reports the native balance of the PoolManager, as does any contract that returns the balance of its argument (src/test/NativeERC20.sol:46-48 models one). Such a face gives a credit only for value that arrives while it is synced, and its transfer function cannot move native funds of the PoolManager.',
  'eth_call with state overrides on https://forno.celo.org, https://rpc.mainnet.arc.io and https://polygon.drpc.org on 2026-11-23, script faces.py, results raw/faces_42220.json, raw/faces_5042.json, raw/faces_137.json and raw/deleg_token.json in ' + W + '; src/PoolManager.sol:349-365')

c(ALL, 'L1',
  'A take of a currency address without code succeeds because the transfer accepts empty return data (src/types/Currency.sol:64-74), while src/interfaces/IPoolManager.sol:174 says take reverts if the amount is not available. What can an ordinary account do with that, and can a pool, a claim or a debt exist under such an address.',
  'The account can make take report success without any transfer, at its own cost. On all 21 deployed PoolManagers, inside one unlock: take of 5 units of a codeless address returned success, the caller delta slot read minus 5 and the delta counter 1. sync of that address reverted with empty data, because balanceOf is a high level call whose empty answer cannot be decoded (src/types/Currency.sol:94). settle with nothing synced paid 0. mint of 7 units added a debt of 7 and a claim of 7. clear reverted with MustClearExactPositiveDelta. A separate unlock that only took 5 units reverted with CurrencyNotSettled, and one that took amount 0 closed, on all 21 networks. The ecrecover address 0x01 gave the same answers as the codeless address. For the identity precompile 0x04 take reverted and sync stored a constant, so the next settle paid 0. Under a codeless id a positive delta could come only from settle, from burn, from a pool or from a hook delta. settle is closed because sync reverts. burn needs a claim, and a claim needs a positive delta to pay for the mint. Pool amounts are rounded against the caller (an add owes the amount rounded up and a removal returns it rounded down, src/libraries/SqrtPriceMath.sol:261-288 and src/libraries/Pool.sol:206-237) and a hook delta is subtracted from the caller delta (src/libraries/Hooks.sol:230, 242 and 312), so the deltas of all accounts in that currency sum to zero or less. No claim and no debt therefore exists under a codeless id. A pool that names it can be initialized (src/PoolManager.sol:117-142) and can hold only positions that need the other currency alone. The only party that loses from the silent take is the caller that asked for it. A holder of a real credit could lose it this way only if the code of a token disappeared after deposits, which needs a token built for that, for example an account whose owner clears its EIP-7702 delegation. That is the pool creator class of F-013.',
  RPC21 + ', scripts tests.py (real_script) and codeless_close.py, results raw/tests_<chainId>.json, raw/compare.json and raw/codeless_close.json in ' + W + '; src/types/Currency.sol:55-96; src/PoolManager.sol:279-336')

c([56, 8453, 4217], 'L6',
  'Lead L-423, second item: which addresses change from no code to code at a scheduled fork or on demand, what do they answer to the call shapes of take and sync before and after, and can a pool, a claim or a debt exist under them before. Enshrined token ranges on BNB Smart Chain (Jenner), Base (B20) and Tempo (TIP-20).',
  'BNB Smart Chain: the client sets JennerTime for mainnet to 1796697000, which is 2026-12-08 02:30 UTC, and from that time resolves every address of the CAS20 family from its prefix (params/config.go line 256, core/vm/evm.go lines 46 to 58). At block 135,275,526 the addresses 0xca52000000000000000001217954ab5467577565, 0xca5200000000000000000069e5bbadfec8b9075a and 0xca52000000000000000000000000000000abcdef held no code and answered balanceOf, transfer of 0, transfer of 1 and empty calldata from the PoolManager with success and no data. Until the fork a take that names such an address succeeds and moves nothing and sync reverts, as for any codeless address. On the Chapel testnet, where the fork has been active since 2026-10-29, the first two addresses hold the one byte code 0xef and answer balanceOf with 32 bytes, and the third, which was never created, reverted on all four shapes at block 144,409,446. Base and Tempo show the same state after activation today. Created B20 and TIP-20 tokens hold the code 0xef. The B20 range addresses 0xb200000000000000000000000000000000abcdef and 0xb2000000000000000000000000000000deadbeef, which hold no token, reverted on all four shapes at block 61,242,809, and the TIP-20 range addresses 0x20c00000000000000000000000000000deadbeef and 0x20c0000000000000000000000000000000abcdef reverted with Uninitialized at block 50,432,878. The address 0x20c0ffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffff lies outside the token id range and answers as a codeless account. By the item above no claim, debt or funded position can exist under an address while it is codeless, so nothing is carried over an activation. A pool can be initialized on a future token address at a price of the creator choice, which is the same on Ethereum for any address that can be predicted. Not confirmed: that BNB Smart Chain mainnet will treat never created CAS20 addresses as Chapel does, because the fork is not active there yet.',
  'https://raw.githubusercontent.com/bnb-chain/bsc/master/params/config.go and https://raw.githubusercontent.com/bnb-chain/bsc/master/core/vm/evm.go fetched 2026-11-23; eth_getCode and eth_call with the PoolManager as sender on https://bsc-dataseed1.bnbchain.org, https://bsc-testnet-dataseed.bnbchain.org (Chapel, chain id 97), https://mainnet.base.org/ and https://rpc.mainnet.tempo.xyz, script enshrined.py, results raw/enshrined.json in ' + W)

c(ALL, 'L1',
  'unlock, take, settle, settleFor, mint, burn, clear and sync carry no noDelegateCall, while the test double puts the modifier on six of them (src/test/ProxyPoolManager.sol:58, 151-183). Lead L-423, third item: call them through DELEGATECALL from a stub and say what an account that points its EIP-7702 delegation at the PoolManager exposes.',
  'A delegatecall runs these functions against the storage, the transient storage and the balance of the calling account, and nothing the PoolManager holds is reached. Test on all 21 deployed PoolManagers, one eth_call each with state overrides: a stub P that forwards every call to the PoolManager by DELEGATECALL, and a funded stub E as the ordinary account. Outside an unlock P.sync succeeded, P.settle reverted with ManagerLocked, P.initialize reverted with DelegateCallNotAllowed, and P.owner and P.protocolFeeController returned zero. E then called P.unlock. Inside the callback P.take of 1000 wei of native currency paid the recipient out of the balance of P. The delta of minus 1000 and the unlocked flag sat in the transient storage of P, while the flag and the delta of the PoolManager read 0. P.mint and P.burn moved a claim in the storage of P, while balanceOf at the PoolManager for the same account read 0. P.settle with 1000 wei returned 1000, P.swap reverted with DelegateCallNotAllowed, and P.sync and P.clear ran. The unlock closed, the native balance of the PoolManager was the same before and after, and a second unlock that only took reverted with CurrencyNotSettled. An account D that carries the designator 0xef0100 followed by the PoolManager address gave the same 29 answers on 20 networks. The Avalanche endpoint (avalanchego 1.15.3) executed the designator as code and failed with invalid opcode 0xef, so a delegated account could not be tested there. What such an account exposes: any caller can borrow its whole native and token balance for the length of one unlock and must return it, a caller can book as its own payment any balance rise it can trigger at the account between sync and settle, and anyone can write claim and operator entries into the storage of the account. owner and protocolFeeController read zero there, initialize, swap, modifyLiquidity and donate revert, and no protocol fee can accrue, so collectProtocolFees has nothing to pay. The exposure is limited to the account that made the delegation. The tests of the repository need the production functions without the modifier, because the double forwards take, settle, mint, burn and clear by delegatecall and expects DelegateCallNotAllowed only from modifyLiquidity, swap and donate (src/test/ProxyPoolManager.sol:151-183, test/NoDelegateCall.t.sol:63-82).',
  RPC21 + ', script tests.py (deleg_script with stubs P and D), results raw/tests_<chainId>.json and raw/compare.json in ' + W + '; src/PoolManager.sol:104-114, 117, 148, 190, 259, 291-336; src/NoDelegateCall.sol:24-32')

c([8453, 4217, 5042, 42220], 'L4',
  'The reverse of the missing noDelegateCall: a contract that the PoolManager has called forwards transfer and balanceOf to an enshrined token by DELEGATECALL or CALLCODE. Does the token act with the PoolManager as holder, and what does an account whose EIP-7702 designator names such a token answer. Base and Tempo at the releases that run today (standing lead L-406), with the delegated account part also on Arc and Celo.',
  'No. With the measuring stub at the PoolManager address as outer caller and a forwarder stub in between, transfer(sink, 1) and balanceOf sent by DELEGATECALL and by CALLCODE reverted with DelegateCallNotAllowed and both balances stayed unchanged, for pathUSD 0x20c0000000000000000000000000000000000000 on Tempo (block 50,433,802, client tempo/v1.19.0-697ea97) and for the B20 tokens 0xb2000000000000000000002feb517dfec7415344 and 0xb2000000000000000000000d8ce462e99ee7a47b on Base (blocks 61,245,309 and 61,245,310, client base/v1.5.6). The direct transfer of 1 in the same call lowered the PoolManager balance by 1 and raised the sink by 1. An account whose designator names one of these tokens, or the Tempo addresses 0xdec0000000000000000000000000000000000000 and 0xfeec000000000000000000000000000000000000, fails every call with an undefined opcode, because the code stored at an enshrined address is the single byte 0xef. The same holds for a created CAS20 token on the Chapel testnet. A take that names such an account reverts. On Arc an account whose designator names 0x3600000000000000000000000000000000000000 answered balanceOf, transfer and empty calldata with success and no data and moved nothing, so it behaves as a codeless currency. On Celo an account whose designator names the CELO token reverted with No Implementation set. None of these accounts is a second id over a balance that the PoolManager holds. BNB Smart Chain mainnet was not run because CAS20 is not active there.',
  'eth_call with state overrides on https://rpc.mainnet.tempo.xyz, https://mainnet.base.org/, https://rpc.mainnet.arc.io, https://forno.celo.org and https://bsc-testnet-dataseed.bnbchain.org on 2026-11-23, scripts inherited.py and deleg_token.py, results raw/inherited.json, raw/deleg_token.json and raw/deleg_token_single.json in ' + W + '; src/PoolManager.sol:110; src/libraries/Hooks.sol:134; src/types/Currency.sol:48, 73')

c(ALL, 'L2',
  'settle and settleFor credit msg.value when no currency is synced and read no balance (src/PoolManager.sol:352-354). Does the deployed code credit exactly the value sent, does the guard at line 356 hold, and did this unit find a frame that carries value nobody paid.',
  'On all 21 deployed PoolManagers a settle with 1000 wei and nothing synced credited 1000 under address(0), the delta slot read 1000, and a native take of 1000 closed the unlock. With a token synced the same call reverted with NonzeroNativeValue. A zero value settle then paid 0 and cleared the slot, and value was accepted again. On Tempo the test ran only because the stub balance came from a state override: the BALANCE opcode returned 0 for the PoolManager there, while eth_getBalance returns one placeholder number for every address. A DELEGATECALL into settle keeps the value of the outer call and credits it in the context of the calling account only (item on noDelegateCall). The one frame on the 21 networks that reports a value nobody paid is the system caller rule that F-058 records for BNB Smart Chain, and the units of cycle 3 walked the transaction types of every network for it. This unit found no further source.',
  RPC21 + ', script tests.py, results raw/tests_<chainId>.json in ' + W + '; src/PoolManager.sol:300-307, 349-365')

c(ALL, 'L1',
  'fromId drops the upper 96 bits of a claim id (src/types/Currency.sol:114-118) and mint and burn accept any uint256 id (src/PoolManager.sol:322-336). Can an id with high bits reach another balance, allowance or currency.',
  'No. On all 21 deployed PoolManagers a mint with the id (0xabc << 160) | X raised the claim balance at the plain id X by the amount and left the balance at the raw id at 0. A burn with other high bits lowered the balance at X, and the delta moved under currency X both times. The source uses the truncated currency for the delta and toId of it for the claim and for the allowance check (src/PoolManager.sol:324-327 and 333-335, src/ERC6909Claims.sol:13-22), and the interface states the truncation (src/interfaces/IPoolManager.sol:200-201 and 208-209). transfer, transferFrom and approve use the raw id, so a balance at an id with high bits can never be minted and every transfer of it fails on the checked subtraction (src/ERC6909.sol:26 and 41). The id never reaches a call: mint and burn make no external call, and the currency that sync, take and initialize receive is an address type.',
  RPC21 + ', script tests.py, results raw/tests_<chainId>.json in ' + W + '; src/types/Currency.sol:110-118; src/PoolManager.sol:322-336; test/types/Currency.t.sol:118-120')

c(ALL, 'L2',
  'Caller identities that a network creates, against the functions that trust msg.sender without a lock or a role: unlock, sync, the ERC-6909 functions, and the controller check, which the zero address passes on 13 networks.',
  'unlock from an address without code reverted with empty data on all 21 networks, because the answer of the callback cannot be decoded (src/PoolManager.sol:110). The zero address holds no code on any of the 21 networks, so no contract code and no delegation can act from it. Its nonce is nonzero on nine OP Stack networks (10 on OP Mainnet, Zora and World Chain, 8 on Base, 7 on Unichain, Ink and Soneium, 5 on Celo, 2 on X Layer) and zero on the other twelve. On seven of the nine (Unichain, Zora, World Chain, Ink, Soneium, Celo, X Layer) protocolFeeController is the zero address. This unit did not trace which transactions raised the nonce. The open leads L-100 and L-129 ask that for upgrade deposits, and the nonce values belong with them. Arc: the CallFrom precompile lets two helper contracts name the sender of a child call. Both helpers pass their own msg.sender (Memo.sol line 43, Multicall3From.sol lines 43, 58, 81 and 161), and the client rejects a named sender that is neither the calling helper nor the transaction signer (crates/evm/src/evm.rs lines 946 to 955) and rejects every scheme other than CALL (lines 895 to 903). A contract that runs in the middle of another account transaction therefore cannot reach unlock or the ERC-6909 functions as that account.',
  'eth_getCode, eth_getTransactionCount and eth_call on the 21 rpc URLs of research/network-risk/state/networks.json on 2026-11-23, results raw/tests_<chainId>.json in ' + W + '; https://raw.githubusercontent.com/circlefin/arc-node/main/contracts/src/memo/Memo.sol, https://raw.githubusercontent.com/circlefin/arc-node/main/contracts/src/batch/Multicall3From.sol and https://raw.githubusercontent.com/circlefin/arc-node/main/crates/evm/src/evm.rs fetched 2026-11-23; src/PoolManager.sol:104-114; src/ProtocolFees.sol:36, 48')

c(ALL, 'L1',
  'Every external function of src/ against its comment in src/interfaces and the tests that name it: further places where the source allows more than the comment or the test describes.',
  'I read the 33 external functions of the PoolManager and its parents against IPoolManager.sol, IProtocolFees.sol, IExtsload.sol, IExttload.sol and the ERC-6909 interface, and against test/PoolManager.t.sol:917-1039, test/Sync.t.sol, test/PoolManager.clear.t.sol and test/NoDelegateCall.t.sol. Beyond the five places of the task and the items of F-006 and F-054 the source allows the following, and none of them lets an ordinary account take or lock funds of others. take with amount 0 debits nothing (src/PoolManager.sol:368-369) and still calls transfer(to, 0) on any address or sends empty calldata to any recipient (src/types/Currency.sol:44-80). Earlier cycles recorded this as levers P1 and P2 and swept special addresses and pooled tokens. settle and settleFor accept any caller while the manager is unlocked and settleFor credits any recipient (src/PoolManager.sol:300-307). An unwanted credit costs its payer and can make another account unlock revert only if the payer code runs inside that unlock. mint credits a claim to any address at the cost of the caller (322-329). clear with amount 0 on a zero delta returns without effect (310-319, test/PoolManager.clear.t.sol:114-135). collectProtocolFees treats amount 0 as the whole accrued amount and pays any recipient the controller names (src/ProtocolFees.sol:54-56). extsload with nSlots 0 reads one slot and returns an empty array (src/Extsload.sol:18-39). initialize makes no check that a currency or a hook has code (src/PoolManager.sol:117-142). updateDynamicLPFee and the three owner and controller functions check only msg.sender. I also recomputed the four transient slot constants from their stated preimages and the selectors used in the tests of this unit with a keccak implementation, and counted the width of every all ones mask in the non test sources. All match.',
  'src/PoolManager.sol:96-142, 279-346; src/ProtocolFees.sol:29-57; src/ERC6909.sol:25-64; src/Extsload.sol:10-63; src/Exttload.sol:10-39; src/interfaces/IPoolManager.sol:109-216; src/interfaces/IProtocolFees.sol:25-51; ' + W + '/keccak.py and lib.py')

c(ALL, 'L1',
  'Walk of assumptions A1 to A14 of ASSUMPTIONS.md against the five loose places of this unit: which assumption does each place lean on, and which stay untouched.',
  'sync without a lock leans on A1 and A2. Inside one transaction it gives the revert recorded in F-071 and no credit, and a transaction boundary that kept transient storage would add the booking of unsolicited inflows that the cycle 2 item on a stale sync describes. The take of a codeless currency leans on A4 and A7 and costs only the caller. The functions without noDelegateCall lean on A8, and the tests on 21 networks show address(this), the storage and the balance of the calling account in use. settle crediting msg.value leans on A3, where the guard at src/PoolManager.sol:356 held on the three networks with a native face and F-058 records the one frame with unpaid value. fromId touches no assumption about the chain. A12 meets these places only through the controller check, treated in the item on caller identities. A5, A6, A9, A10, A11, A13 and A14 do not meet any of the five places: none of them holds a balance for another party, adds a transfer, loops, uses an opcode outside the list of A10, or depends on ordering.',
  'research/network-risk/ASSUMPTIONS.md; the other cleared items of this file; src/PoolManager.sol:279-365')

